{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794602337,"nonce":"JwW3s/ylMK+/ydGkQp8Fo14PqME=","message":"Fixed in fbe71924ea4e95c54b17ad9a7ad8a135ac485162. The abort was the glyph buffer, not the primitive buffer: render_text wrote every glyph of the frame (246,950 of them, 23,707,200 bytes) into a buffer of max_glyphs (50,000 at 96 bytes, 4,800,000 bytes), and the glass upload had the same gap. Both clip to the configured maximum with a warning, as write_primitives_safe does. The frame in the reproducer (20,000 flat rows, BLINC_MOTION_PROMOTION=1) now logs \"Glyph count 246950 exceeds buffer capacity 50000, truncating\" and runs.\n\nA renderer test (capacity_tests) draws 40 glyphs into a renderer of 8; it fails with the same overrun message without the fix.\n\nThe 240-byte figure in the description was the glyph stride at 50,000 glyphs, not the primitive stride. Text past the capacity is dropped, so a baked frame that large shows part of its text; the real fix for such a frame is that it should not paint every row (a list with a viewport culls, see 0e62d2e).","files":null}]}